    "status": "confirmed",
    "reasoning": "Both earlier answers are confirmed by their respective captured worked solutions. A point (a,b) lies on y=arcsin(x) exactly when a=sin(b) and b is in [−π/2,π/2]. For q-65744, point I fails because sin(1/2)<1/2<π/6; point II satisfies sin(π/3)=√3/2 with π/3 in the principal range; point III has ordinate 2π outside that range. For q-117012, point I fails because sin(√2/2)<√2/2<π/4; point II satisfies sin(−π/3)=−√3/2 with −π/3 in the principal range; point III has ordinate 5π/4 outside that range. Thus II only is mathematically correct and source-supported for each question.",
